#!/usr/bin/env python3

# Run a git command and paint its output.
#
#   git-color status
#   git-color push
#   git-color log --oneline -20
#
# Every argument is handed to git unchanged. git's own coloring is turned off
# (and any escape sequence that still shows up is stripped), so the whole
# output is repainted from one palette: paths and refs are drawn as inline code
# blocks the way glow renders `code` and the way the cli-helpers prompt draws
# quoted spans, hashes, URLs, commit messages, statistics and status words each
# get their own color, arrows become real arrows and the punctuation between
# them fades back.
#
# The palette is resolved per role from GIT_COLOR_<ROLE>, then from the shared
# COLOR_* theme variables of cli-helpers, then from the built-in default, so a
# themed shell stays in charge of the look.

